# The volcano: why the soap matters
#
# A baking-soda volcano without washing-up liquid is a fizz. With it, the
# gas is caught in a film as it comes out and the whole thing climbs over
# the rim. Nothing about the chemistry changes — the same carbon dioxide,
# the same amount of it. What changes is whether the gas can get away.
register lv2

# 1. The control. Soda and vinegar, no soap: it fizzes, the gas leaves, and
#    there is nothing to see afterwards but a flat liquid.
add v1 NaHCO3 5g
add v1 white_vinegar_5_percent 50mL
look v1

# 2. The same reaction with washing-up liquid already in the glass. Put the
#    soap in FIRST — it has to be there when the gas arrives, because gas
#    that has already left cannot be caught.
new beaker
add v2 dish_soap 5mL
add v2 NaHCO3 5g
add v2 white_vinegar_5_percent 50mL
look v2

# 3. And it does not last. Foam drains and the bubbles join up; wait a
#    couple of minutes and the same vessel is most of the way back to a
#    liquid with a head on it.
wait 2min
look v2

# What this does not model: bubble size, how the film drains, or the
# difference between a detergent film and a protein one. The half-life is
# the recipe's own reviewed number and the trapped fraction is a bounded
# teaching value — this says THAT it foams and roughly how fast it goes,
# not what the foam is made of.
